The stats table used hardcoded column widths, so a note longer than 30
characters overflowed its column and broke the borders.  Rows are now
collected first, the widest label, value and note in each column fix
the column widths, and the separators and buffer are sized from the
same measurements, so the borders always line up and the output can
never be truncated no matter how the numbers or descriptions grow or
shrink.  The climbs-per-wakeup annotation shows N/A when no wakeup
decays have been recorded instead of dividing by an implicit one.

